※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4211

※ Spreading a tuple decides how many arguments are handed over before anything
※ counts them, so a tuple with the wrong number of members is reported as the
※ wrong number of arguments.  That is the same complaint the call would draw if
※ its arguments had been written out one at a time, which is the point: the
※ glyph is a shorter way of writing them, not a different kind of call.

fn two(a: u8, b: u8) → u8:
    a + b

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let three: 〈u8, u8, u8〉 = 〈1u8, 2u8, 3u8〉
    two(⁂three)
